course: dictionary
lesson: 2156
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: The same word can mean different things. You work out which meaning is right by reading the words around it. Cork can be a material, a stopper, a verb, or a place. «The bubbles in sparkling wine are made by yeast; their corks fly out hard» is about the stopper that closes the bottle, because bottles and bubbles are the clues.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. «The Dead Sea is so salty that people float on it like corks» is not about a bottle at all. Floating like a cork means floating high and easily on the water, so the clue is the sea. Ask yourself: which meaning fits here? Then swap that meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense.
explain: Capital letters are clues too. «A teacher in Cork gave her class three tests for any prank» uses a capital C, so Cork is a place: a city and county in the south of Ireland. Some words are also verbs, so «to cork a bottle» means to close it with a cork. The word is the same, but the job it does in the sentence changes.
fact: Cork is a light, soft, springy material made from the thick bark of the cork oak tree.
fact: A cork is also a stopper that closes a bottle.
fact: As a verb, to cork means to close a bottle with a cork.
fact: Cork is a city and county in the south of Ireland, and it starts with a capital letter.
fact: Cork floats because it is less dense than water, so «people float on it like corks» in the salty Dead Sea.
exercise: «The bubbles in sparkling wine are made by yeast; their corks fly out hard.» What is a cork here? | A tree that grows in Portugal | A loud noise from a party | A small fish that swims in the sea | *A stopper that closes a bottle
exercise: «A teacher in Cork gave her class three tests for any prank.» What is Cork here? | A stopper for a bottle | To close something up tightly | *A city and county in the south of Ireland | A light material from tree bark
exercise: Ellie says, «Please cork the bottle when you finish.» What does cork mean here? | To pop a bottle open | To wash a bottle out | *To close a bottle with a cork | To float on top of water
test: Which thing is made from the bark of a tree? | Glass | Wool | Metal | *Cork
test: «The Dead Sea is so salty that people float on it like corks.» What does like corks show? | *Floating high and easily on the water | Being very hot | Sinking to the bottom | Making a loud noise
exam: Which sentence uses cork as a stopper? | Cork is a city in the south of Ireland. | The cork oak grows mainly in Portugal and Spain. | Granda's wake was held in Cork last week. | *Pop the cork and pour the drinks.
